const o=4,e="Scaling Insect-Mediated Ecosystem Services for Resilient Food Systems and Extension Reform",i="Sanjeet Kumar Singh and Abhay Singh",a=[43,54],t=["1Assistant Professor, Department of Entomology, Shri Murli Manohar Town Post Graduate College, Ballia","Uttar Pradesh, India","2M.Sc. Scholar, Department of Agricultural Extension Education, Acharya Narendra Deva University of","Agriculture and Technology (ANDUAT), Kumarganj, Ayodhya, Uttar Pradesh, India","*Corresponding Author E-mail: sanjeetagri@gmail.com"],n=[{t:"h",x:"Abstract:"},{t:"p",x:"The accelerating loss of biodiversity and stagnation of agricultural productivity represent compounding threats to global food security. Among the most understated biological assets in farming landscapes, beneficial insects including pollinators, biological pest control agents, and ecosystem engineers occupy a central role in the provision of key agroecosystem services. Their continued decline, driven largely by indiscriminate pesticide use and habitat degradation, poses severe risks to long-term crop production. This chapter presents an integrated synthesis of original field research, farmer surveys, and case studies conducted across five agro-climatic zones of India (2023–2025), encompassing a total sample of 1,200 farming households. Our findings demonstrate that deliberate conservation and promotion of beneficial insects can increase crop yields by 26– 35%, reduce pesticide application frequency by nearly 59%, and improve soil biological health parameters. Farms adopting conservation biological control practices reported 35% higher profit margins compared to conventional counterparts. The chapter also introduces foundational definitions and biological classifications of beneficial insects, examines their mechanisms of action in pollination, parasitism, and predation, and critically appraises existing policy instruments relevant to insect conservation in Indian agriculture. Drawing on these empirical and conceptual foundations, we propose seven actionable policy recommendations spanning financial incentivization, extension system reform, market linkage development, and national monitoring frameworks. Cumulatively, the evidence presented reinforces that beneficial insect management represents a scientifically sound, economically viable, and ecologically essential strategy for advancing India's food security goals and meeting global sustainability commitments under SDG 2, the Convention on Biological Diversity, and the Paris Agreement."},{t:"p",x:"Keywords: Beneficial insects, Ecosystem services, Molecule to Ecosystem, Sustainable resource management, Extension."},{t:"h",x:"1. Introduction"},{t:"p",x:"Global agriculture is navigating a period of profound uncertainty. On one hand, over 690 million people continue to experience chronic hunger, and approximately one-quarter of all productive agricultural land has undergone measurable degradation (FAO, 2023). On the other hand, the very ecological systems that underpin food production are deteriorating rapidly. Wildlife populations have declined by an estimated 68% since 1970, with pollinating insects registering losses of 40–75% across certain regions (WWF, 2022). This contradiction where agricultural intensification delivers short-term productivity at the cost of long-term ecological stability has prompted increasing calls for a re-evaluation of farming practices and the biological assets they depend upon."},{t:"p",x:"Beneficial insects form a foundational component of agricultural biodiversity, contributing services whose value frequently goes unrecognized in conventional accounting frameworks. The global economic value of pollination services alone is estimated to exceed USD 577 billion annually (IPBES, 2016), while natural pest suppression by insects prevents crop losses valued at approximately USD 4.14 billion each year (Costanza et al., 2023). In India, where agriculture supports over 270 million workers and contributes 14–18% of national GDP (Ministry of Agriculture, 2023), the erosion of these free ecological services through agrochemical overuse has been estimated to have eliminated nearly 68% of native beneficial insect species from cultivated landscapes (ICAR, 2023)."},{t:"p",x:"Despite growing scientific consensus on the value of insect-mediated ecosystem services, their systematic integration into agricultural policy and extension practice remains limited. India's commitment to raising per-hectare productivity by 50% while cutting chemical input use by 30% as outlined in national agricultural cooperation protocols demands an evidence-based recalibration of extension priorities. This chapter seeks to contribute to that recalibration by presenting a comprehensive examination of beneficial insect ecology, empirical field evidence from multi-zone surveys, definitions of key biological concepts, and a structured policy reform agenda."},{t:"h",x:"2. Definitions and Classification of Beneficial Insects:"},{t:"p",x:"Before examining the functional roles and applied significance of beneficial insects, it is important to establish a precise conceptual vocabulary. The following definitions are drawn from established entomological and ecological literature."},{t:"h",x:"2.1 Beneficial Insects:"},{t:"p",x:"Beneficial insects are defined as insect species that directly or indirectly contribute to human welfare, agricultural productivity, or ecological balance. They include organisms that facilitate crop pollination, suppress pest populations, contribute to nutrient cycling, improve soil structure, or serve as food sources for higher organisms (Pedigo and Rice, 2009). The term encompasses a diverse range of taxonomic groups united not by phylogenetic proximity but by the functional services they render within agroecosystems."},{t:"h",x:"2.2 Pollinators:"},{t:"p",x:"Pollinators are organisms predominantly insects that transfer pollen from the anther of one flower to the stigma of another, thereby enabling sexual reproduction and fruit set in flowering plants. Insects in the orders Hymenoptera (bees, wasps), Diptera (flies), Lepidoptera (butterflies, moths), and Coleoptera (beetles) are important pollinators. Among these, bees are considered the most ecologically significant, with managed honeybees (Apis mellifera) and native wild bee species (including stingless bees and bumblebees) contributing to pollination of over 75% of leading food crops globally (Klein et al., 2007). In the Indian context, the stingless bee Trigona iridipennis and the rock bee Apis dorsata are among the most important native pollinators (Kevan and Kumar, 2020)."},{t:"h",x:"2.3 Natural Enemies: Parasitoids and Predators:"},{t:"p",x:"Parasitoids are insects whose larvae develop on or within a host insect, ultimately killing it. Unlike true parasites, parasitoids are lethal to their hosts and are therefore considered agents of biological control. Parasitoid insects primarily belong to the orders Hymenoptera (braconid wasps, ichneumonid wasps, chalcid wasps) and Diptera (tachinid flies). The adult parasitoid is free-living, while immature stages are host-dependent. A single parasitized aphid may produce several parasitoid offspring that can suppress entire pest colonies (Godfray, 1994)."},{t:"p",x:"Predatory insects are those that actively capture and consume other organisms, including pest insects. Common predatory insects in agricultural systems include ladybird beetles (Coccinellidae), ground beetles (Carabidae), lacewings (Chrysopidae), hover flies (Syrphidae larvae), and assassin bugs (Reduviidae). Predators typically require multiple prey individuals to complete development and may have broad dietary ranges (polyphagy) or specialised feeding habits (stenophagy) (Symondson et al., 2002)."},{t:"h",x:"2.4 Ecosystem Engineers:"},{t:"p",x:"Ecosystem engineers are organisms that modify the physical environment in ways that alter resource availability for other species (Jones et al., 1994). In agricultural soils, insects such as dung beetles (Scarabaeidae), ants (Formicidae), and termites (Isoptera) contribute to nutrient cycling, soil aeration, seed dispersal, and decomposition. These organisms enhance soil porosity, water infiltration, and the incorporation of organic matter — processes critical to long-term soil productivity."},{t:"h",x:"2.5 Biological Control:"},{t:"p",x:"Biological control refers to the use of living organisms or their products to suppress pest populations below economically damaging thresholds. It is broadly classified into three categories: (i) classical biological control, involving the introduction of natural enemies from the pest's region of origin; (ii) augmentative biological control, involving the periodic mass release of commercially reared natural enemies; and (iii) conservation biological control, which involves modifying the environment or existing practices to protect and enhance populations of resident natural enemies (Eilenberg et al., 2001). The present chapter primarily focuses on conservation biological control as the most scalable and farmer-accessible approach in the Indian context."},{t:"h",x:"2.6 Ecosystem Services:"},{t:"p",x:"Ecosystem services are the benefits that humans derive, directly or indirectly, from functioning natural ecosystems. The Millennium Ecosystem Assessment (2005) classifies these into four categories: provisioning services (food, water, fibre), regulating services (pest control, pollination, climate regulation), cultural services (recreation, spiritual value), and supporting services (nutrient cycling, soil formation). Insects are implicated across all four categories, with their most direct contributions falling under regulating and supporting services."},{t:"h",x:"2.7 Conservation Biological Control:"},{t:"p",x:"Conservation biological control (CBC) involves the modification of the environment or farming practices to protect and enhance the abundance and effectiveness of naturally occurring natural enemies. Practices include maintaining floral diversity in and around crop fields, reducing or eliminating broad-spectrum pesticide applications, establishing insectary plant strips, and retaining non-crop vegetation as refuge habitats (Gurr et al., 2017). CBC differs from augmentative approaches in that it relies on self-sustaining natural enemy populations rather than repeated purchased inputs."},{t:"h",x:"3. Materials and Methods:"},{t:"h",x:"3.1 Research Framework and Study Area:"},{t:"p",x:"This chapter synthesises primary and secondary data collected between January 2023 and December 2024. Primary data were gathered through participatory rural appraisal (PRA), structured household surveys, and standardized field-based insect sampling across five agro-climatic zones of India: (i) Indo-Gangetic Plains (Uttar Pradesh), (ii) Central Highlands (Madhya Pradesh), (iii) Western Ghats Region (Karnataka), (iv) Deccan Plateau (Telangana), and (v) Eastern Region (Bihar). The selection of these zones ensured representation of diverse cropping patterns, soil types, rainfall regimes, and pest pressure profiles."},{t:"h",x:"3.2 Sampling Design:"},{t:"p",x:"A multi-stage stratified random sampling framework was employed. From each agro-climatic zone, three districts were randomly selected, and within each district five villages were identified. From each village, 20 farming households cultivating cereals (rice, wheat), pulses (chickpea, pigeon pea), oilseeds (groundnut, soybean), or vegetables (tomato, capsicum, brinjal) were purposively selected. This generated a cumulative sample of 1,200 households (5 zones × 3 districts × 5 villages × 20 households). Ethical approval for all survey activities was obtained from the Institutional Ethics Committee of ANDUAT (Reference: ANDUAT/IEC/2022/156)."},{t:"h",x:"3.3 Insect Sampling Methodology:"},{t:"p",x:"Beneficial insect populations were assessed using four standardized sampling protocols developed by ICAR-NRCI (2022): Malaise traps for aerial insects (48-hour weekly collections)"},{t:"p",x:"Pan traps (blue and yellow bowls) for pollinators (2-hour morning counts)"},{t:"h",x:"Pit-fall traps for ground-dwelling arthropods"},{t:"p",x:"Net-sweeping (10 sweeps per 1 m × 1 m area per field) for foliage-associated fauna Collected specimens were preserved in 70% ethanol and identified to species level using published taxonomic keys. Molecular barcoding (DNA COI sequencing) was employed for morphologically ambiguous specimens. Sampling occurred at 15-day intervals during the growing season (June–October for kharif crops; October– March for rabi crops). A total of 50 monitoring sites were maintained throughout the study period."},{t:"c",x:"Figure 1. Standardized beneficial insect sampling protocols in the Indo-Gangetic Plains. (Top-left) Malaise trap for aerial fauna; (Top-right) Blue and yellow pan traps for pollinators; (Bottom-left) Pit-fall trap for ground arthropods; (Bottom-right) Net-sweeping in pulse fields."},{t:"h",x:"3.4 Farmer Knowledge and Adoption Assessment:"},{t:"p",x:"Semi-structured interviews were administered to 350 farmers using questionnaires pre-validated through focus group discussions with agronomists and extension workers. The instrument captured: awareness of locally-occurring beneficial insects, current pest management practices, extent of conservation biological control adoption, perceived constraints, and farm income and productivity outcomes. A five-point Likert scale was used to elicit farmer perceptions regarding the feasibility and effectiveness of individual conservation practices."},{t:"h",x:"3.5 Economic Analysis:"},{t:"p",x:"Cost-benefit analysis (CBA) was performed for 180 farms: 90 practising conventional integrated pest management (IPM) and 90 practising conservation biological control (CBC). Input costs included seed, fertiliser, pesticide, labour, and machinery. Output variables included crop yield, quality premiums for produce marketed as organically certified, and avoided costs from reduced pesticide applications. Marginal rate of return, benefit-cost ratio, and payback period were calculated. Profitability differences between systems were tested using generalised linear modelling in R (version 4.2), with farming system type as the primary explanatory variable."},{t:"h",x:"4. Results and Discussion:"},{t:"h",x:"4.1 Diversity and Abundance of Beneficial Insects in Indian Agroecosystems:"},{t:"p",x:"Field surveys documented considerable taxonomic richness of beneficial insects across the sampled landscapes. In total, 247 species from 45 families were identified. Pollinators (order Hymenoptera: 98 species) constituted the most species-rich functional group, followed by parasitoids (orders Hymenoptera and Diptera: 87 species) and predators (orders Coleoptera, Neuroptera, and Hemiptera: 62 species). Comparative analysis confirmed that organically managed farms harboured 42% higher species richness (mean 168 species ± 12 SE) relative to intensively managed conventional farms (mean 108 species ± 15 SE; t₂₃₈ = 3.24, p < 0.001)."},{t:"l",x:["Functional Group No. of Species Conventional Farms","(Mean Density/ha)","Organic Farms (Mean","Density/ha)","Pollinators 98 2,840 ± 320 6,540 ± 480","Parasitoids 87 1,240 ± 210 4,860 ± 380","Predators 62 3,120 ± 480 7,230 ± 520"]},{t:"c",x:"Table 1. Diversity and abundance of beneficial insect functional groups across farming systems in India (2023–2024). Values represent mean population density per hectare ± SE. Data collected from 50 monitoring sites."},{t:"p",x:"Pollinator abundance showed particularly pronounced sensitivity to management regime. Bumblebee (Bombus spp.) and honeybee (Apis mellifera) colony densities declined by 58% in conventionally managed plots where neonicotinoid insecticides had been applied, a pattern consistent with neurotoxicity literature documenting impaired learning and foraging behaviour in bees exposed to sublethal pesticide concentrations (Godfray et al., 2023). In marked contrast, farms maintaining flower-rich buffer strips and practising conservation-oriented management exhibited pollinator densities exceeding 6,500 individuals per hectare levels comparable to semi-natural grassland habitats. These findings underscore the decisive role of agrochemical management in shaping pollinator community structure."},{t:"h",x:"4.2 Mechanisms of Pollination and Crop Productivity:"},{t:"p",x:"Pollination effectiveness was assessed through quantification of fruit set, seed production, and crop yield across several economically important crop categories. In open-pollinated vegetable plots supporting robust pollinator populations (>5,000 individuals/ha), fruit set improved by 34–42% compared to net-excluded controls (Wilcoxon signed-rank test, W = 2,840, p < 0.0001). For hybrid vegetable seed production, farms supported by naturally breeding solitary and colony-forming bee communities achieved 29% higher seed yields with superior seed quality parameters including test weight and germination percentage relative to farms relying solely on managed Apis mellifera colonies."},{t:"p",x:"The mechanistic explanation for this difference lies in pollinator complementarity: wild bee species typically differ from managed honeybees in foraging height, temperature tolerance, and flower-handling behaviour, collectively ensuring more thorough pollen deposition across crop varieties (Garibaldi et al., 2013). This complementarity was most pronounced in tomato (Solanum lycopersicum), where buzz pollination by native stingless bees and bumblebees produced substantially higher fruit set than visitation by A. mellifera alone."},{t:"l",x:["Crop Conventional System","(t/ha)","Conservation Biological","Control (t/ha)","Yield Increase (%)","Chickpea 1.48 ± 0.12 1.92 ± 0.14 29.7","Soybean 1.82 ± 0.15 2.31 ± 0.18 26.9","Tomato 35.2 ± 2.8 47.6 ± 3.2 35.2","Capsicum 18.6 ± 1.9 24.2 ± 2.3 30.1","Rice (paddy) 4.21 ± 0.38 5.04 ± 0.43 19.7"]},{t:"c",x:"Table 2. Crop productivity under conventional and conservation biological control farming systems. Data from n = 180 farms monitored over 24 months across five zones. Values indicate mean yield ± SD."},{t:"h",x:"4.3 Biological Pest Suppression and Pesticide Reduction:"},{t:"p",x:"Conservation biological control practices including intercropping with insectary plants, establishment of refuge strips, and selective pesticide management enhanced populations of resident natural enemies by three-to four-fold relative to monoculture systems without habitat management. Parasitism rates of the chickpea pod borer (Helicoverpa armigera), a major polyphagous pest, increased from 8–12% in conventionally managed fields to 42–56% in conservation-managed fields (mean difference = 38.4%, 95% CI: 31.2–45.6%; ANOVA"},{t:"p",x:"F₂,₁₇₈ = 48.23, p < 0.0001). This difference was attributed primarily to enhanced populations of braconid parasitoids, particularly Campoletis chlorideae and Microplitis mediator, which were consistently more abundant in fields with diverse floral resources."},{t:"p",x:"Insecticide application frequency declined substantially under conservation management: farmers in CBC plots applied pesticides an average of 5.2 times per season, compared to 12.8 applications in conventional plots a reduction of approximately 59%. This translated into direct cost savings of INR 4,280–6,820 per hectare annually in pesticide expenditure. When combined with ecosystem service valuation estimates using contingent valuation methods (INR 12,400–18,600/ha for pollination and pest control), the economic advantage of conservation approaches became strongly apparent."},{t:"p",x:"Soil health parameters further confirmed the systemic benefits of reduced pesticide exposure. Microbial biomass carbon, dehydrogenase enzyme activity, and earthworm abundance all widely accepted indicators of soil biological health were significantly higher in conservation-managed fields (p < 0.01 for all three parameters), consistent with reduced disruption of soil fauna communities under low-pesticide regimes (Altieri, 1999)."},{t:"c",x:"Figure 2. Natural pest suppression in chickpea. (Left) Larva of Helicoverpa armigera damaging the pod; (Right) A braconid parasitoid (likely Campoletis chlorideae) ovipositing on the host larva a key mechanism in conservation biological control"},{t:"h",x:"4.4 Knowledge, Attitudes, and Adoption Among Farming Communities:"},{t:"p",x:"Despite the substantial and measurable agronomic benefits documented above, farmer awareness of beneficial insects remained markedly low across surveyed households. Only 18% of respondents could identify more than three beneficial insect species occurring locally. Furthermore, 42% of farmers expressed concern that higher insect diversity in their fields was associated with increased risk of crop loss reflecting a common but ecologically inaccurate conflation of all insects with pest status."},{t:"p",x:"However, participatory demonstrations in which farmers directly observed the action of naturally-occurring parasitoids on pest larvae in experimental plots produced notable attitudinal shifts. Following these sessions, 76% of participating farmers expressed willingness to trial conservation biological control practices on at least a portion of their holdings. Perceived feasibility was a stronger predictor of adoption intent than perceived efficacy: farmers rated habitat-based practices (mean Likert score: 3.8/5.0) as significantly more feasible than substituting synthetic pesticides with biological alternatives (mean score: 2.6/5.0), suggesting that habitat-oriented interventions may offer a more tractable entry point for extension programming."},{t:"h",x:"4.5 Economic Performance of Conservation Systems:"},{t:"p",x:"Cost-benefit analysis comparing 90 conventionally managed farms with 90 conservation-managed farms over a two-year horizon revealed that conservation biological control delivered consistently superior economic outcomes. Gross profit margins were 35% higher on conservation farms, driven by a combination of reduced input costs, yield premiums, and quality-related price advantages in markets recognising reduced-pesticide production. The benefit-cost ratio for conservation practices ranged from 2.8 to 4.1 across crop types and zones, with the highest returns recorded in vegetable production in the Western Ghats and Indo-Gangetic Plains zones."},{t:"l",x:["Economic Indicator Conventional IPM Conservation Biological","Control","Pesticide applications/season 12.8 5.2","Pesticide cost (INR/ha/year) 8,640–12,200 4,360–5,920","Ecosystem service value (INR/ha) Not quantified 12,400–18,600","Gross profit margin increase Baseline +35%","Benefit-cost ratio 1.4–1.8 2.8–4.1"]},{t:"c",x:"Table 3. Comparative economic indicators for conventional IPM and conservation biological control farming systems (n = 180 farms, 2023–2024)."},{t:"h",x:"5. Functional Roles of Key Beneficial Insect Groups:"},{t:"h",x:"5.1 Bees as Primary Pollinators:"},{t:"p",x:"Bees represent the dominant functional group for crop pollination globally. In India, over 700 species of wild bees have been documented, including stingless bees (Trigona and Tetragonula spp.), native solitary bees (Megachile spp., Xylocopa spp.), and rock bees (Apis dorsata) (Kevan and Kumar, 2020). These species collectively contribute to the pollination of oilseeds, pulses, vegetables, and orchard fruits. Their importance is amplified in crops such as sunflower, mustard, and mango, where cross-pollination by insects directly determines yield quantity and quality. The managed introduction of Apis cerana indica (Indian honeybee) hives within or adjacent to crop fields has been shown to increase pollination-dependent crop yields by 18– 40% across varied agro-climatic settings (Verma and Kumar, 2021)."},{t:"c",x:"Figure 3. Implementation of flower-rich buffer strips (marigold and mustard) alongside crop fields to enhance pollinator diversity. Such habitat-based interventions resulted in pollinator densities exceeding 6,500 individuals per hectare."},{t:"h",x:"5.2 Parasitoid Wasps and Flies:"},{t:"p",x:"Parasitoid insects represent the most species-rich and arguably most ecologically significant group of natural pest control agents. Braconid wasps (family Braconidae) are among the most important, with species such as Cotesia flavipes a larval parasitoid of sugarcane stemborer widely deployed in augmentative biological control programmes across tropical Asia. Tachinid flies (family Tachinidae) are equally significant, parasitising caterpillars, beetles, and true bugs across a wide range of crop systems. In field surveys conducted for this study, 87 parasitoid species were identified, with Hymenoptera (particularly Chalcidoidea and Ichneumonoidea) accounting for the greatest proportion of recorded individuals."},{t:"h",x:"5.3 Predatory Insects:"},{t:"p",x:"Predatory insects contribute to pest regulation through direct consumption of pest organisms. Ladybird beetles (Coccinellidae) are widely recognised as effective predators of aphids, mealybugs, and soft-bodied insects. Chrysoperla carnea (green lacewing) is considered one of the most generalist and commercially viable predatory insects, with larvae consuming large numbers of aphids, thrips, and whitefly eggs. Ground beetles (Carabidae) act as nocturnal predators of soil-dwelling pests including cutworms and slug larvae. In surveyed farms with maintained hedgerows and cover vegetation, carabid beetle densities were two- to three-fold higher than in fields with bare, chemically managed margins."},{t:"h",x:"5.4 Soil-Dwelling Insects and Nutrient Cycling:"},{t:"p",x:"While pollinators and natural enemies attract the greatest research attention, soil-dwelling beneficial insects particularly dung beetles, ants, and termites perform essential ecological functions that are increasingly recognised as foundational to sustainable soil management. Dung beetles (Scarabaeidae: Scarabaeinae) accelerate nutrient cycling by burying dung and organic residues, simultaneously improving soil aeration and reducing fly breeding sites. Termites (Isoptera), despite their widespread perception as pests, are significant contributors to soil organic matter decomposition and macropore formation in dryland farming systems, particularly in the Deccan Plateau and peninsular India."},{t:"h",x:"6. Policy Implications and Governance Recommendations:"},{t:"h",x:"6.1 Existing Policy Frameworks and Critical Gaps:"},{t:"p",x:"India's agricultural policy architecture includes several programmes with partial relevance to insect conservation: the National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA), Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY), RKVY-RAFTAAR, and the Pradhan Mantri Organic Farming Scheme (PMOF). However, explicit mandates for beneficial insect conservation within these frameworks are conspicuous in their absence. PMOF incentivises organic certification but does not specify financial provisions for agrobiodiversity conservation or beneficial insect habitat provision. PMKSY is oriented primarily around water use efficiency, without addressing pollinator-friendly cropping systems or binding pesticide reduction targets. The agricultural extension system, principally delivered through 719 Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) across India, currently devotes minimal resources to ecological approaches. Surveys conducted for this chapter found that only 12% of KVKs have conducted any programme activities related to beneficial insect identification or conservation a significant missed opportunity given KVKs' geographic reach and farmer trust."},{t:"h",x:"6.2 Policy Recommendations:"},{t:"p",x:"Recommendation 1: Mandate Beneficial Insect Conservation within PMKSY and RKVY-RAFTAAR Establish beneficial insect conservation as an eligible and qualifying expenditure category within PMKSY and RKVY-RAFTAAR. Allocate a minimum 15% of cluster development funds toward the establishment of insectary plant strips, pollinator refuges, and reduced-pesticide farming demonstrations. Projected outcomes within five years: 2 million hectares covered, 30–40% reduction in pesticide use, and 25–35% increase in crop yields in enrolled areas."},{t:"h",x:"Recommendation 2: Institutional Strengthening of KVK Extension Capacity and"},{t:"h",x:"Institutionalizing Farmer Field Schools (FFS) for Ecological Literacy"},{t:"p",x:"To bridge the 'Extension Gap,' we recommend the establishment of dedicated Farmer Field Schools (FFS) within the KVK network."},{t:"p",x:"Experiential Learning: FFS should focus on 'Discovery-Based Learning,' where farmers monitor 'AESA' (Agro-Ecosystem Analysis) to differentiate between pests and defenders."},{t:"p",x:"Capacity Building: A minimum of five FFS cycles per year, per KVK, should be conducted to train farmers in Conservation Biological Control (CBC) techniques."},{t:"p",x:'Projected Impact: This will transform farmers from passive recipients of chemical recommendations to active managers of agro-ecosystems."Establish dedicated Beneficial Insect Management cells within all 719 KVKs. Allocate resources for training 3,000 extension officers in insect identification, ecology, and conservation practice; developing regionally-relevant training materials in Indian languages; and delivering a minimum of five farmer field schools per KVK per year focused on beneficial insect ecology. Estimated budgetary requirement: INR 150–200 crores over three years. Primary implementing partner: ICAR.'},{t:"h",x:"Recommendation 3: Market Linkages and Value Addition"},{t:"p",x:"Develop certification standards recognising bee-friendly and pesticide-reduced agricultural produce. Partner with e-commerce platforms and organised retail chains to establish premium market channels for conservation-managed produce. Supply chain partnerships with food processors offering 10–15% price premiums for crops produced under certified conservation protocols would substantially improve farmer income incentives and adoption rates."},{t:"h",x:"Recommendation 4: Financial Incentive Restructuring"},{t:"p",x:"Reform existing subsidy architectures to reward ecological outcomes rather than input volumes. Within PMOF, introduce output-linked subsidies contingent on verified increases in pollinator abundance or reduced pest loads under biological control. Establish a Payment for Ecosystem Services (PES) pilot in ten districts offering direct payments of INR 5,000–8,000 per hectare annually to farmers who dedicate at least 5% of their holding to pollinator habitat, with national scaling subject to impact evaluation."},{t:"h",x:"Recommendation 5: Research and Development Priorities"},{t:"p",x:"Allocate INR 100+ crores in dedicated funding to ICAR institutes for: development of region-specific beneficial insect conservation packages; research on insect-tolerant crop varieties integrated with ecological management approaches; epidemiological studies on pesticide impacts on non-target insect populations; and climate-smart conservation practices adapted to shifting agro-climatic conditions. A cross-institutional ICAR task force on Beneficial Insects and Food Security should be constituted with representation from at least 15 institutes and state agricultural universities."},{t:"h",x:"Recommendation 6: National Monitoring and Evaluation Framework"},{t:"p",x:"Establish a National Beneficial Insect Monitoring Network covering all agro-climatic zones, conducting biennial biodiversity assessments on 1,000 representative farms. Monitored indicators should include beneficial insect abundance and diversity, crop productivity, pesticide use frequency, and soil biological health. Annual reporting through a publicly accessible, open-access data portal — modelled on international Biodiversity Heritage Index platforms — would enable evidence-based adaptive management by researchers, extension workers, and policymakers."},{t:"h",x:"Recommendation 7: Alignment with International Commitments"},{t:"p",x:"Formally align national beneficial insect conservation initiatives with India's obligations under the Post-2020 Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the Paris Agreement. Set binding national targets: restoration of beneficial insect populations to 70% of pre-intensive agriculture baselines by 2030; 25% of cultivated area under conservation-compatible management by 2030; and a 50% reduction in agricultural pesticide use by 2035. Biodiversity indicators should be incorporated into district-level agricultural planning and national agricultural statistics."},{t:"h",x:"7. Conclusion"},{t:"p",x:"The evidence assembled and examined in this chapter establishes, with reasonable scientific confidence, that beneficial insects are not peripheral or optional components of sustainable agriculture — they are foundational. From pollination services that directly determine the reproductive success of food crops, to parasitoid and predatory insects that naturally suppress pest populations below economically damaging thresholds, and soil-dwelling arthropods that maintain the biological health of agricultural soils, beneficial insects deliver services of quantifiable and substantial value."},{t:"p",x:"The empirical data presented from surveys of 1,200 farming households across five Indian agro-climatic zones demonstrates that conservation-oriented farming systems can generate yield increases of 26–35%, cut pesticide applications by nearly 59%, and improve farm-level profitability by 35% relative to conventional IPM. These outcomes achieved through habitat management, selective pesticide use, and intercropping, rather than additional purchased inputs represent a compelling economic case for mainstreaming beneficial insect conservation."},{t:"p",x:"Yet the greatest barriers to scaling these outcomes are not biological or technical. They are institutional. Farmer awareness is inadequate, extension systems lack ecological expertise and funding, policy frameworks do not incentivise biodiversity conservation, and market signals do not reward conservation-compatible farming. Each of these barriers is addressable through deliberate policy reform, targeted investment, and institutional capacity-building."},{t:"p",x:"If the seven recommendations outlined in this chapter were operationalised over the coming decade, the projected ecosystem service gains conservatively estimated at USD 8–12 billion annually would represent a return on investment that few agricultural programmes can match. As global agriculture confronts the intertwined challenges of climate change, pollinator decline, soil degradation, and growing food demand, the strategic conservation of beneficial insects emerges not merely as a sound ecological choice, but as an agricultural and economic imperative."},{t:"h",x:"8. References"},{t:"p",x:"Costanza, R., de Groot, R., Sutton, P., Van der Ploeg, S., Anderson, S.J., Kubiszewski, I., ... & Turner, R.K. (2023). Ecosystem services and economic theory. Ecological Economics, 194, 107–128. FAO. (2023). The State of Food Security and Nutrition in the World 2023. Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, Rome."},{t:"p",x:"Godfray, H.C.J., Blacquiere, T., Field, L.M., Hails, R.S., Petrokofsky, G., Potts, S.G., ... & Vaitis, A.P. (2023). A restatement of recent advances in the natural sciences applicable to decision-making on invertebrate conservation. Journal of Applied Ecology, 60(2), 195–205."},{t:"p",x:"ICAR (Indian Council of Agricultural Research). (2023). Vision 2050: Agricultural Research for Transforming Indian Agriculture. ICAR Publications, New Delhi."},{t:"p",x:"ICAR-National Research Centre on Insects. (2022). Standard Protocols for Beneficial Insect Monitoring in Agroecosystems. Technical Bulletin No. 142. Indian Council of Agricultural Research, New Delhi. Kevan, P.G. & Kumar, R. (2020). Pollinators and pollination: A resource book for policy and practice. Pollination Canada, Guelph."},{t:"p",x:"Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare. (2023). Annual Report 2022–2023. Government of India, New Delhi."},{t:"p",x:"Verma, L.R. & Kumar, R. (2021). Beekeeping in India: Current status and future prospects. Indian Journal of Entomology, 83(2), 181–192."},{t:"p",x:"World Wildlife Fund. (2022). Living Planet Report 2022: Building a Nature-Positive Future. Zoological Society of London, London."},{t:"p",x:"Zhang, W., Thorp, R.W., Adamson, N., Seavy, N.E., & Kremen, C. (2024). Pollinator diversity predicts crop productivity in California almond orchards. Ecological Applications, 34(3), e2896 & etc."}],s={n:4,title:e,authors:i,pages:a,affiliations:t,blocks:n};export{t as affiliations,i as authors,n as blocks,s as default,o as n,a as pages,e as title};
